                <pb facs="0883" n="797" sameAs="frus:frus1903/pg797"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Bowen to Mr. Hay.</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Telegram.—Paraphrase.]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Caracas</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1902-12-13"><hi rendition="#italic">December 13,
                        1902</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p>(Mr. Bowen reports that the situation at Caracas is much quieter; that the
                    excitement was caused by the precipitate departure of the British and German
                    ministers, by the arrest of all British and German subjects in Venezuela, and by
                    the capture of Venezuelan naval vessels without declaration of war or
                    blockade.)</p>
            </div>
        </body>
